84375, -0.0036258697509765625, -0.0029315948486328125, -0.01277923583984375, -0.00527191162109375, -0.006256103515625, 0.01434326171875, 0.00214385986328125, -0.0024280548095703125, 0.022125244140625, -0.00640869140625, -0.023651123046875, 0.01036834716796875, -0.00811004638671875, 0.02325439453125, 0.0269317626953125, -0.04559326171875, -0.010467529296875, -0.0006246566772460938, 0.01290130615234375, -0.0230560302734375, 0.006305694580078125, 0.00591278076171875, 0.01141357421875, 0.021942138671875, -0.03924560546875, -0.00450897216796875, -0.0205535888671875, -0.020721435546875, 0.019012451171875, -0.021453857421875, 0.017181396484375, -0.0484619140625, 0.0196380615234375, -0.006183624267578125, 0.002124786376953125, -0.0136871337890625, 0.00922393798828125, -0.0100555419921875, 0.00507354736328125], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "**\n1. Introduction and Main Results\n**\n\nModeling spatial or temporal point processes is a central problem in statistics, with applications in ecology, epidemiology, and neuroscience. In many settings, observed event locations are influenced by both known covariates (structured, interpretable effects) and unknown, potentially complex spatial or temporal trends (flexible, data-driven structure). Semiparametric models, which combine fixed effects for covariates with nonparametric components, offer a principled way to capture both sources of variation. While Cox processes with Gaussian process (GP) intensities have been widely studied, the permanental process\u2014a Cox process with intensity modeled via the square of a GP\u2014has unique mathematical and computational properties ([McCullagh & M\u00f8ller, 2006]; [Shirai & Takahashi, 2003]). However, prior work has largely focused on purely nonparametric intensity modeling, leaving open the question of how to rigorously incorporate fixed effects and understand their interplay with nonparametric structure in permanental processes.\n\nThis work addresses this gap by developing a semiparametric framework for permanental processes that unifies fixed effects and nonparametric structure, clarifies the regularization properties, and provides practical guidance for estimation and interpretation.\n\n**Key Contributions:**\n1. We provide the first rigorous mathematical decomposition of the permanental process intensity into fixed effects and a nonparametric RKHS component, clarifying the regularization structure in the diffuse prior limit.\n2. We consolidate and precisely define the assumptions under which this decomposition is unique and well-posed, and discuss the implications of assumption violations with concrete examples.\n3. We demonstrate, via simulation, the practical estimation and interpretability benefits of this semiparametric approach.\n4. We discuss computational challenges and provide guidance on scalable inference for permanental processes, linking practical considerations to the theoretical results.\n\nOur main mathematical result is as follows:\n\n**Proposition 1.** *Let \\( \\lambda(x) \\) denote the intensity function of the permanental process, modeled as \\( \\log \\lambda(x) = \\beta^T z(x) + f(x) \\), where \\( z(x) \\) are known covariates, \\( \\beta \\) are fixed effects coefficients, and \\( f \\) lies in an RKHS \\( \\mathcal{H}_k \\) with kernel \\( k \\). Assume:*\n\n1. *(A1) The likelihood \\( \\ell(f, \\beta) = \\log p(\\text{data} \\mid f, \\beta) \\) is differentiable and strictly convex in \\( (f, \\beta) \\); that is, for any two distinct pairs \\( (f_1, \\beta_1) \\neq (f_2, \\beta_2) \\), the function \\( t \\mapsto \\ell(tf_1 + (1-t)f_2, t\\beta_1 + (1-t)\\beta_2) \\) is strictly convex for \\( t \\in (0,1) \\).*\n2. *(A2) The observed data locations \\( x_i \\) are fixed and known.*\n3. *(A3) The kernel \\( k \\) is positive definite, so the RKHS norm \\( \\|f\\|_{\\mathcal{H}_k} \\) is a true norm.*\n4. *(A4) The prior on \\( \\beta \\) is Gaussian with covariance \\( \\tau^2 I \\), and \\( \\tau^2 \\to \\infty \\) (the diffuse prior limit), so the prior becomes non-informative.*\n\n*Then the penalized likelihood estimator for \\( f \\) and \\( \\beta \\) is equivalent to minimizing the negative log-likelihood plus \\( \\|f\\|_{\\mathcal{H}_k}^2 \\), with no penalty on \\( \\beta \\). In this setting, the decomposition of the intensity into fixed and nonparametric components is unique.*\n\n*Proof Sketch.* The penalized likelihood estimator minimizes\n\\[\nL(f, \\beta) = -\\log p(\\text{data} \\mid f, \\beta) + \\|f\\|_{\\mathcal{H}_k}^2 + \\frac{1}{2\\tau^2}\\|\\beta\\|^2.\n\\]\nAs \\( \\tau^2 \\to \\infty \\), the penalty on \\( \\beta \\) vanishes, so \\( \\beta \\) is estimated solely from the likelihood. The RKHS prior on \\( f \\) induces the penalty \\( \\|f\\|_{\\mathcal{H}_k}^2 \\). By (A1), the objective is strictly convex in \\( (f, \\beta) \\), ensuring a unique minimizer. By (A3), the RKHS norm is a true norm, and the representer theorem ([Wahba, 1990]) applies: the minimizer \\( f^* \\) can be written as \\( f^*(x) = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\alpha_i k(x, x_i) \\) for some coefficients \\( \\alpha_i \\). Uniqueness of the decomposition follows from strict convexity and the positive definiteness of \\( k \\). If (A3) fails (e.g., \\( k \\) is only positive semi-definite), the RKHS norm may be degenerate, and non-uniqueness or identifiability issues can arise (see Section 2 for examples). If (A1) fails (e.g., due to collinearity among covariates), multiple minimizers may exist. See Appendix A for a detailed proof.\n\n**\n2. Model Structure, Assumptions, and the Diffuse Prior Limit\n**\n\nA *penalized likelihood estimator* is the minimizer of the sum of the negative log-likelihood and a regularization term, which controls model complexity and prevents overfitting ([Wahba, 1990]). The *diffuse prior limit* refers to the case where the prior variance \\( \\tau^2 \\) on \\( \\beta \\) tends to infinity, making the prior non-informative; thus, \\( \\beta \\) is estimated purely from the likelihood, while regularization applies only to \\( f \\).\n\nThe assumptions in Proposition 1 are necessary for both mathematical tractability and practical estimation:\n- **(A1) Differentiability and strict convexity** of the likelihood ensure a unique minimizer and well-behaved optimization. For standard point process likelihoods (e.g., Poisson, permanental process likelihoods), these conditions typically hold. However, strict convexity may fail if covariates are collinear (i.e., some \\( z(x) \\) are linearly dependent), if the model is misspecified, or if the number of parameters exceeds the number of data points. For example, if \\( z(x) \\) is constant across all \\( x \\), \\( \\beta \\) is not identifiable. In such cases, estimation may be unstable or non-unique, and additional regularization or dimensionality reduction may be required ([Hastie et al., 2009]).\n- **(A2) Fixed data locations** \\( x_i \\) is standard in spatial point process modeling, as the observed points are treated as fixed realizations.\n- **(A3) Positive definiteness of the kernel** \\( k \\) is required for the RKHS structure and for the representer theorem to hold. If \\( k \\) is only positive semi-definite, the RKHS norm may be degenerate, leading to non-uniqueness or identifiability problems. For example, if \\( k \\) is the zero kernel, any function in the null space has zero penalty, so the decomposition is not unique. In such cases, adding a small diagonal \"nugget\" to the kernel can restore positive definiteness ([Rasmussen & Williams, 2006]).\n- **(A4) Diffuse prior on \\( \\beta \\):** If a finite prior variance is used, a penalty remains on \\( \\beta \\), which can help with identifiability in high-dimensional settings or when covariates are highly correlated.\n\n**\n3. Representer Theorem and Decomposition\n**\n\nThe representer theorem ([Wahba, 1990]) states that the solution to regularized empirical risk minimization problems in an RKHS can be written as a finite linear combination of kernel functions evaluated at the observed data points. In our context, this means that the optimal \\( f \\) minimizing the penalized likelihood (with penalty \\( \\|f\\|_{\\mathcal{H}_k}^2 \\)) can be expressed as\n\\[\nf^*(x) = \\sum_{i=1}^n \\alpha_i k(x, x_i),\n\\]\nwhere \\( x_i \\) are the observed data locations and \\( \\alpha_i \\) are coefficients determined by the data and the fixed effects. This finite representation is crucial for computational tractability. If the kernel is not positive definite or the likelihood is not strictly convex, the representer theorem may not apply, and the solution may not admit such a representation, potentially leading to identifiability or optimization issues. For example, with a degenerate kernel, the solution may not be unique, and with non-strictly convex likelihood (e.g., collinear covariates), multiple minimizers may exist.\n\n**\n4. RKHS Penalty, Kernel Choice, and Practical Considerations\n**\n\nThe squared RKHS norm \\( \\|f\\|_{\\mathcal{H}_k}^2 \\) serves as the regularization penalty for the nonparametric component. For example, with a squared exponential kernel, this penalty favors smooth functions. The choice of kernel directly impacts the flexibility and smoothness of the nonparametric term; for instance, a Mat\u00e9rn kernel allows for varying degrees of smoothness. Kernel selection can be guided by"}}